# Schema: verify-exposure.sh
#
# Documents the verification script so an agent unfamiliar with it can run
# and interpret it.

## Purpose

Proves A-2 (and the per-hostname half of Phase 3/4 verification): every
exposed hostname actually resolves on the tailnet and answers over the
configured protocol. It turns "I think the services are exposed" into a
pass/fail per hostname.

## Interface

- Invocation: `verify-exposure.sh [--http] <TAILNET> <name>...` or
  `verify-exposure.sh --from-list <TAILNET> <list-file>...`
- Must run from a tailnet-connected device (the only vantage where
  `<name>.<TAILNET>` resolves per D-7).
- `--from-list` extracts the top-level keys of each list file — the keys
  ARE the exposed hostnames under the files contract, so the check can't
  drift from the config.
- `--http` switches the probe to `http://<name>` for P-2 = off
  deployments (R-2 degrades to plain HTTP on the tailnet).
- Exit 0: every hostname answered. Exit 1: at least one failed. Exit 2:
  usage error.

## Interpretation

| Output | Meaning |
|--------|---------|
| `OK https://<name>.<TAILNET>` | TLS handshake + HTTP(S) HEAD succeeded — the proxy is up and reachable for that hostname |
| `FAIL https://<name>.<TAILNET>` | resolution, TLS, or connect failure — proxy down, hostname not registered, name not resolving, or P-2/protocol mismatch |

## Rules the script follows

- POSIX sh + curl only; no hardcoded hostnames, keys, or paths — the
  script is fully argument-driven and contains no deployment data.
- Read-only: it only probes; it never mutates the proxy, tailnet, or
  config.
- The HEAD request (`-I`) is deliberate: it exercises the full path
  (TLS → proxy → target) without transferring service bodies.